    <body>
      <sec id="article-36421.s1" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>The anterior, or ventral, white commissure is a collection of nerve fibers that cross the midline of the spinal cord, transmitting information to and from the contralateral side of the brain. The structure extends along the entire length of the spinal cord and lies posterior to the anterior median fissure. Key pathways that decussate via the anterior white commissure include 2nd-order neurons of the spinothalamic tract and upper motor neurons of the anterior corticospinal tract. Crossing fibers confer functional significance to the anterior white commissure as a conduit for communication between the brain and the contralateral body in both sensory and motor pathways (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Spinal Cord Cross-Sectional Anatomy).</p>
        <p>The anterior white commissure is clinically significant because lesions or compression in this region, such as in central cord syndrome or syringomyelia, result in characteristic deficits in pain and temperature sensation. Surgical interventions, including commissural myelotomy or punctate myelotomy, target the anterior white commissure to disrupt nociceptive pathways and alleviate intractable pain. A broad grasp of&#x000a0;the anatomy and function of the anterior white commissure allows clinicians to localize lesions accurately, plan interventions safely, and predict sensory and motor outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-36421.s2" sec-type="Structure and Function">
        <title>Structure and Function</title>
        <p>A cross-section of the spinal cord reveals a butterfly-shaped region of gray matter, composed of nerve cell bodies, surrounded by white matter containing myelinated nerve fibers. The anterior white commissure is a rectangular collection of nerve fibers situated directly anterior to the central canal and gray commissure, and posterior to the anterior median fissure. Sensory fibers of the spinothalamic tract and motor fibers of the anterior corticospinal tract decussate within the anterior white commissure. Crossing fibers confer functional importance to the anterior white commissure in the transmission of contralateral sensory and motor information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>The spinothalamic tract constitutes a major ascending neural pathway for the sensory detection of pain, temperature, touch, and pressure (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Ascending Sensory Pathways).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r2">[2]</xref> Sensory fibers synapse on cell bodies within the dorsal root ganglion. Axons of 2nd-order neurons from the spinothalamic tract decussate at the anterior white commissure and ascend 1 to 3 spinal cord levels through the Lissauer tract. After decussation, fibers continue to the ventroposterolateral nucleus of the thalamus, where synapses occur. Third-order neurons transmit sensory information to the primary somatosensory cortex.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>The corticospinal tract constitutes a major descending neural pathway for the control of voluntary movement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r4">[4]</xref> Most fibers of the corticospinal tract decussate in the medulla, forming the lateral corticospinal tract (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Lateral Corticospinal Tract Anatomy). Up to 15% of fibers within the anterior corticospinal tract decussate at the anterior white commissure. These fibers synapse with the cell bodies of lower motor neurons in the gray matter of the contralateral ventral horn.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r5">[5]</xref> Fibers of the anterior corticospinal tract innervate axial muscles of the trunk.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-36421.s3" sec-type="Embryology">
        <title>Embryology</title>
        <p>At approximately week 3 following fertilization, the developing embryo forms the neural groove, a shallow median indentation of the neural plate. Lateral margins of the neural plate elevate and fuse to form the neural tube. Signaling from the notochord directs the neural tube and associated structures to differentiate into components of the central nervous system. The brain develops from the cranial end of the neural tube, whereas the spinal cord arises from the caudal end. Neural crest cells originate from the neural ectoderm during neurulation, undergo a transition from epithelial to mesenchymal cells, migrate, and give rise to the peripheral nervous system.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r8">[8]</xref> The anterior white commissure develops as a central nervous system structure, composed of spinal cord white matter and derived embryologically from the neuroectoderm.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-36421.s4" sec-type="Blood Supply and Lymphatics">
        <title>Blood Supply and Lymphatics</title>
        <p>Blood supply to the spinal cord arises from 3 primary vessels: the anterior spinal artery and the paired posterior spinal arteries.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r9">[9]</xref> The anterior spinal artery originates from the vertebral artery and from radicular arteries, the most prominent of which is the artery of Adamkiewicz. The anterior spinal artery supplies the anterior white commissure.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-36421.s5" sec-type="Physiologic Variants">
        <title>Physiologic Variants</title>
        <p>Physiological variations of the anterior white commissure depend on differences between the spinothalamic and anterior corticospinal tracts. Variations in these tracts can result in differing numbers of fibers from each tract crossing within the anterior white commissure. The anterior corticospinal tract exhibits marked physiological variability among individuals. In a study of 50 spinal cords, 74% demonstrated asymmetry in the size of the right and left corticospinal tracts, with most cords containing a greater number of fibers on the right side.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r10">[10]</xref> The anterior corticospinal tract develops later than the lateral corticospinal tract and may be absent in certain individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r11">[11]</xref> Consequently, crossing fibers within the anterior white commissure may lack contributions from the anterior corticospinal tract in some individuals.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-36421.s6" sec-type="Surgical Considerations">
        <title>Surgical Considerations</title>
        <p>Commissural myelotomy is a surgical procedure involving an incision in the anterior white commissure to disrupt the spinothalamic tract. The procedure interrupts pain transmission within the spinothalamic tract, producing bilateral analgesia. In a study of 11 patients, commissural myelotomy significantly improved oncogenic pain in 8 patients with abdominal and pelvic involvement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r12">[12]</xref> Punctate myelotomy, or limited myelotomy, represents an alternative approach for interrupting nociceptive pathways. Rather than incising the anterior white commissure, punctate myelotomy disrupts visceral nociceptive signals via an incision in the ventromedial dorsal columns of the spinal cord.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-36421.s7" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>The anterior white commissure constitutes a clinically significant region in central cord syndrome, an incomplete injury that typically involves the cervical spinal cord and affects the upper extremities more than the lower extremities (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Patterns of Incomplete Spinal Cord Injury).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r14">[14]</xref> Central cord syndrome often results from acute-on-chronic trauma, such as falls or motor vehicle collisions, particularly in adults older than 50.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Syringomyelia, defined as cavitation of the cervical spinal cord originating in the central canal, initially affects crossing spinothalamic fibers within the anterior white commissure, producing a characteristic loss of pain and temperature sensation. Symptoms can progress to a "cape-like" distribution of numbness and weakness as adjacent structures become involved.</p>
        <p>The anterior white commissure contains decussating pain and temperature fibers of the spinothalamic tract. This region represents a potential therapeutic target for interventions aimed at pain control.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-36421.s8" sec-type="Other Issues">
        <title>Other Issues</title>
        <p>The anterior white commissure of the spinal cord should not be confused with a distinct midline neural pathway, the anterior commissure of the brain. The anterior commissure of the brain is a fiber bundle located posterior to the corpus callosum and is believed to facilitate interhemispheric communication.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-36421.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>
