    <back>
      <floats-group id="article-36421.s10">
        <fig id="article-36421.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Spinal Cord Cross-Sectional Anatomy. The diagram displays a transverse view of the spinal cord with its primary anatomical landmarks. Gray matter regions include the posterior, lateral, and anterior horns, while white matter is divided into posterior, lateral, and anterior columns. The anterior white commissure is highlighted in green, marking the area where decussating axons cross to the contralateral side. Image courtesy O.Chaigasame</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="anterior__white__comm" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-36421.s11">
        <fig id="article-36421.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Patterns of Incomplete Spinal Cord Injury. Comparative transverse views demonstrate lesion distributions across 3 common clinical syndromes. A normal spinal cord cross-section is displayed in panel A. Shaded regions indicate the area affected by central cord syndrome in panel B, the area affected by anterior cord syndrome in panel C, and the area affected by Brown-Sequard syndrome in panel D. These visual markers highlight the anatomical basis for the differing sensory and motor deficits observed in clinical practice. Contributed by D Peterson, PhD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Corticospinal__Tract__Lesions" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-36421.s12">
        <fig id="article-36421.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Lateral Corticospinal Tract Anatomy. The schematic illustrates the descending pathway from the motor cortex to the spinal cord. Axons originate in the precentral gyrus, descend through the posterior limb of the internal capsule, and traverse the midbrain, pons, and rostral medulla. Approximately 90% of these axons undergo decussation at the pyramidal level in the medulla before forming the lateral column. This tract is primarily responsible for the execution of skilled movements in the distal extremities, such as the hands and feet. Image courtesy S Bhimji MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="lateralcorticospinal" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-36421.s13">
        <fig id="article-36421.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Ascending Sensory Pathways. The diagram compares the anatomical trajectories of the dorsal column system and the spinothalamic tract. Both pathways utilize a 3-neuron chain to transmit peripheral stimuli to the postcentral gyrus. The dorsal column system facilitates fine touch and proprioception, with decussation occurring in the medulla via the medial lemniscus. In contrast, the spinothalamic tract mediates pain and temperature sensations, with 1st-order neurons synapsing and decussating immediately within the spinal cord. OpenStax College, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1417_Ascending_Pathways_of_Spinal_Cord.jpg">Public Domain,</ext-link> via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="DCMLT" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
